ed a South Asian Union is what I've  been arguing for now 20 years on the  model of the European Union where the  sovereignty of the individual states is  not challenged but there's an opening of  borders there is trade between these two  countries and the years of war are ended  and there is demilitarization - and I  think this could be done and within this  framework if you take Pakistan India Sri  Lanka Bangladesh Nepal you could begin  to solve the contentious issue the Tamil  question in Sri Lanka the Kashmir  question between India and Pakistan by  the Union guaranteeing the autonomy of  these regions not giving them  independence which many of them don't  even want but guaranteeing their  autonomy  and I think that is the way we have to  look if we want to end this century it  for this region at least on a high note  and and what's the current state of  relations between Pakistan and India of  course they've fought several wars  they've been on the brink of war even  recently in this decade both have  nuclear weapons but they seem to have as  well a process of dialogue and and I  think both countries probably understand  the importance of reaching some sort of  Accord is anything like that on the  horizon or is it too far-fetched at this  point well it is on the horizon but  there is there are no real concrete  moves being made in its direction and at  the same time even as we speak the area  of Kashmir occupied by India has  witnessed the largest mass civil  disobedience movement nonviolent ever in  its history demanding an end to the  Indian presence so it's not the Pakistan  is the only country under pressure on  the northwest frontier India is under  pressure with from within Kashmir of  people who say they don't want to be  ruled by India they don't want the  presence of Indian troops in the region  so in this situation it is essential for  both the leaders of both countries and  other countries to come together and  think of a unified solution which you